# ------------------------------------------------------------------ chapters
F(id="command", part=0, title="The Chain of Command Above a Permit",
  kick="The bodies that stand above one factory's permit, from the United Nations down to the national ministry.",
  sum="A factory, a mine or a power plant is usually understood as a local matter. It has a site, a manager and a permit pinned to an office wall. That permit says how much the site may release into the air and the water. But the decisions that shape what the permit allows are connected to a much larger system of treaties, funds, ministries and businesses, most of which the people living next to the site will never hear named.<br><br>At the international level, the UN Environment Programme, based in Nairobi, was created in 1972 to lead on the environment. Separate treaty offices in Bonn, Geneva and Montreal look after the agreements on climate, chemicals, waste and wildlife. The money for those agreements sits mostly in Washington. National ministries sit below these bodies and are formally responsible for the rules inside their own borders. They issue the permits.<br><br>The map follows that chain from the top down, because looking up from the factory gate would hide where many of its limits come from. There is also a striking fact at the start of the story. Since 1972 the number of environmental laws has multiplied many times over. In the same years the pollution those laws were written to limit has grown. The rest of the map is an attempt to explain how both of those things can be true at once.",
  bridge="The slides follow the chain from the first global conference to the ministry that signs a permit. They begin with the body created to lead, then the treaty offices, then the place where the money is kept. The fourth slide reaches the national ministries. The last slide sets the count of laws beside the measure the laws were aimed at, country by country.",
  rec="An agency in Nairobi, treaty offices in Europe, money in Washington and a ministry that signs the permit: that is the chain above every polluting site, and it grew alongside the pollution.",
  out="So the chain runs like this: a programme in Nairobi, treaties serviced from Bonn and Geneva, money held in Washington, and ministries that issue permits. The laws multiplied and so did the emissions. The next chapters look at the top of the chain to see why: what the treaties can actually make a country do, who is in the room when they are written, and which court can overrule them.",
  roles=["Where the international bodies are based"],
  steps=[
   S("1972", "Nairobi", "One Agency, Created to Lead", "The first part of the chain is the agency itself. In 1972, governments met in Stockholm for the first United Nations conference on the environment. They agreed to create a permanent body, the UN Environment Programme, and placed its headquarters in Nairobi, Kenya. Most countries wrote their first modern environmental laws in the years that followed, which is why this map counts from 1972. UNEP can gather evidence, host negotiations and publish reports. It cannot fine a government or close a factory. In 2019 it published the first global review of how environmental law is working, and that review supplies several of the figures used here. The treaties themselves are looked after elsewhere, as the next slide shows.", UNEP19,
     add={"Kenya": 0, "Sweden": 0}, arcs=[("Stockholm 1972", "Nairobi")], pins=[("Nairobi", "UNEP"), ("Stockholm 1972", "Stockholm, 1972")], pl=["unep", "ozone"], ex=["unep2019"]),
   S("1992", "Bonn", "A Separate Office for Each Treaty", "A treaty is a written agreement between countries. Each major environmental treaty has its own small office, called a secretariat, which organises the meetings and collects the reports countries send in. The climate office is in Bonn. The offices for hazardous waste, traded chemicals and endangered species are in Geneva. The office for the treaty on species and ecosystems is in Montreal. Researchers who catalogue these agreements list more than a thousand of them. This matters because no single body is in charge of the whole. Each office answers only to the countries that signed its treaty, and none of them has inspectors or police. What they do have is a calendar of meetings, which later chapters visit.", "https://iea.ulaval.ca/en",
     add={"Germany": 0, "Switzerland": 0, "Canada": 0}, pins=[("Bonn", "Climate"), ("Geneva", "Waste, chemicals, wildlife trade"), ("Montreal", "Biodiversity")], pl=["unfccc", "brs", "cites", "cbd", "ipcc"], z=3.0),
   S("2022", "Washington", "The Money Sits in Washington", "A treaty can set a goal, but it cannot pay for a sewage plant or a forest ranger. The main fund for the environmental treaties is the Global Environment Facility. It is hosted by the World Bank in Washington, which also acts as its trustee, meaning the Bank holds and manages the money. Donor governments pledged 5.3 billion dollars to the fund for the four years from 2022. The Green Climate Fund, the largest fund under the climate treaty, is based in Incheon, South Korea. The same World Bank lends far larger sums for roads, dams, power plants and mines. So the institution that keeps the environmental money is also one of the largest financiers of the projects environmental law exists to control. Chapter 6 returns to how small the environmental funds are next to other public spending.", "https://www.thegef.org/who-we-are/funding",
     add={"United States": 0, "Korea, Rep.": 0}, arcs=[("Washington", "Incheon")], pins=[("Washington", "World Bank, GEF, IFC"), ("Incheon", "Green Climate Fund")], pl=["worldbank", "gef", "ifc", "gcf", "imf"]),
   S("2017", "Every continent", "On Paper, Each Ministry Decides", "Below the international bodies are the national governments. By 2017, 164 countries had a ministry or cabinet-level body for the environment and 176 had a general environmental law. These are the bodies that turn a treaty into national rules and issue the permit for each site. On paper, each one decides for itself. In practice a ministry works inside limits it did not set: the treaties its country has signed, the loans its government has taken, the trade and investment agreements that later chapters describe, and the budget its own finance ministry allows it. The ringed points show the ministries and regulators of the largest economies and the largest resource producers on each continent.", UNEP19,
     pl=["epa_us", "doi_us", "moefcc", "mee_cn", "ibama", "mma_br", "klhk", "eccc", "dcceew", "ea_uk", "bmuv", "moe_jp", "rpn", "nesrea", "semarnat", "dffe", "denr"], ex=["unep2019"], w=1, z=1.2),
   S("1972 to latest", "Every country with figures", "More Laws, More Emissions", "This map compares the two halves of the puzzle. UNEP counted 38 times more environmental laws in 2017 than in 1972. Over the same period the world's yearly carbon dioxide emissions from fuel and industry rose from about 16 billion tonnes to about 39 billion, an increase of 144 per cent. Each country is shaded by how much its own yearly emissions have changed since 1972. They fell in 30 countries and rose in 154. Many of the countries where they fell, including the United Kingdom and Germany, now import goods whose production emits elsewhere. Carbon dioxide is used here because it has been measured for every country for the whole period, not because it is the only thing environmental law is meant to limit.", "https://github.com/owid/co2-data",
     data={"k": "co2_1972", "only": None}, z=1.15, w=1),
  ],
  src=[{"t": "UNEP, Environmental Rule of Law: First Global Report (2019)", "u": UNEP19}, {"t": "International Environmental Agreements Database Project", "u": "https://iea.ulaval.ca/en"}])

F(id="treaties", part=0, title="Treaties That Cannot Make a Country Obey",
  kick="Most environmental treaties have no penalty for breaking them and no cost for leaving.",
  sum="A national law usually has a police force and a court behind it. An international treaty has neither, unless the countries that write it choose to build something similar into the text. Most environmental treaties do not. They set a goal, ask each country to report on its progress, and rely on embarrassment to do the rest.<br><br>This is a choice, and it is made by the governments at the negotiating table. The same governments have signed trade treaties that allow penalties and investment treaties that allow a company to collect money from a country that breaks them. Chapter 4 looks at those. For the environment, the usual design is a promise that each country sets for itself and may leave.<br><br>The connection to the factory permit is direct. A ministry that knows its country can miss a treaty target without cost has little reason to write a strict permit, and a company that knows it has little reason to expect one. One environmental treaty was built differently, with trade penalties and a fund, and it worked. It appears at the end of this chapter and again in the last part of the story, because it shows the weakness of the others was not unavoidable.",
  bridge="The slides take three treaties in the order they were written. The first is the Kyoto Protocol, which set binding targets and lost a member when the target was about to be missed. The second is the Paris Agreement, which let each country choose its own target. The third shows what happens when a treaty needs every country to agree. The last slide is the exception: a treaty with penalties.",
  rec="A climate treaty with targets lost the country about to miss its target; the next let each country set its own; a plastics treaty needed everyone's agreement and got none.",
  out="A treaty that has no penalty can be missed, and a treaty that can be left will be left when it starts to cost something. The one treaty that had a penalty worked. That raises the question of who is in the room when the weaker designs are chosen, which is the next chapter.",
  roles=["Where the treaty was agreed or is serviced", "A country that left or blocked"],
  steps=[
   S("1997 to 2012", "Kyoto", "Kyoto: Binding Targets, and an Exit", "The Kyoto Protocol, agreed in Japan in 1997, was the first treaty to give rich countries numbered targets for cutting greenhouse gases. The United States signed it but never ratified it, which means its legislature never approved it, so it was never bound. Canada did ratify, and then its emissions rose well above its target. In December 2011 Canada announced it was leaving, and the withdrawal took effect a year later, just before its performance would have been judged. Leaving cost Canada nothing. This matters because it showed every other government the real strength of the treaty: a target was binding only for as long as a country chose to stay.", "https://unfccc.int/kyoto_protocol",
     add={"Japan": 0, "Canada": 1, "United States": 1}, arcs=[("Kyoto", "Ottawa"), ("Kyoto", "Washington")], pins=[("Kyoto", "Kyoto Protocol, 1997"), ("Ottawa", "Canada leaves, 2011")], pl=["unfccc", "eccc"]),
   S("2015 to 2026", "Paris", "Paris: Each Country Sets Its Own Target", "The next treaty avoided Kyoto's problem by removing the binding targets. Under the Paris Agreement of 2015, each country writes its own pledge, called a nationally determined contribution, and reports on it. There is no penalty for a weak pledge or a missed one. The United States has now left twice. It gave notice in 2017 and left in 2020, rejoined in 2021, gave notice again in January 2025 and left on 27 January 2026. That month it also announced it would leave the 1992 climate convention itself, the parent of both Kyoto and Paris. For a ministry anywhere else, the lesson is the one Kyoto taught: the largest historical emitter treats the agreement as optional.", "https://unfccc.int/process-and-meetings/the-paris-agreement",
     add={"France": 0, "Germany": 0, "United States": 1}, arcs=[("Paris", "Washington")], pins=[("Paris", "Paris Agreement, 2015"), ("Washington", "Left 2020, rejoined 2021, left 2026")], pl=["unfccc", "epa_us"]),
   S("2022 to 2025", "Geneva", "Plastics: One Objection Is Enough", "In 2022 countries agreed to write a treaty to end plastic pollution. Talks ran through six sessions, from Uruguay to Busan in South Korea, and a final round in Geneva in August 2025. More than a hundred countries wanted the treaty to limit how much plastic is made. A smaller group of oil- and gas-producing countries wanted it to cover only waste. Because these talks worked by consensus, meaning nothing passes if any country objects, the smaller group could not be outvoted. The Geneva session ended without a treaty. Plastic is made from oil and gas, so a limit on plastic is a limit on a product those countries sell. The next chapter counts who else was in the room.", "https://enb.iisd.org/plastic-pollution-marine-environment-negotiating-committee-inc5.2-summary",
     add={"Switzerland": 0, "Korea, Rep.": 0}, arcs=[("Busan", "Geneva")], pins=[("Geneva", "Final session, August 2025: no treaty"), ("Busan", "Fifth session, 2024")], pl=["unep", "acc"], ex=["plastics234"], z=3.0),
   S("1987", "Montreal", "The Exception: A Treaty With Penalties", "One environmental treaty was designed the way trade treaties are. The Montreal Protocol of 1987 dealt with chemicals that were destroying the ozone layer, the part of the atmosphere that blocks harmful sunlight. It banned countries inside the treaty from trading those chemicals with countries outside it, so staying out meant losing a market. It also set up a fund that paid poorer countries the cost of switching. Every country in the world has joined, the only United Nations treaty to achieve that, and about 98 per cent of the chemicals have been phased out. The comparison matters for everything above: where governments wanted a treaty to work, they knew how to write one.", "https://ozone.unep.org/treaties/montreal-protocol",
     add={"Canada": 0, "Kenya": 0}, arcs=[("Montreal", "Nairobi")], pins=[("Montreal", "Montreal Protocol, 1987"), ("Nairobi", "Ozone Secretariat")], pl=["ozone", "unep"]),
  ],
  src=[{"t": "UNFCCC, status of ratification of the Paris Agreement", "u": "https://unfccc.int/process/the-paris-agreement/status-of-ratification"}])

F(id="guests", part=0, title="The Regulated Are in the Room",
  kick="At the talks where limits are negotiated, the industries to be limited send more people than almost any country.",
  sum="The previous chapter showed that most environmental treaties are written without penalties. This chapter looks at who attends the meetings where they are written.<br><br>A United Nations climate summit, known as a COP, is open to more than government negotiators. Trade associations, companies and campaign groups can register as observers, and governments can put anyone they like on their own delegation, including company staff. The United Nations publishes the list of everyone registered. Since 2021 a coalition of campaign groups has read that list line by line and counted the people who work for fossil fuel companies or for trade associations that represent them. A lobbyist is a person paid to influence a decision on behalf of a client or employer.<br><br>The counts do not prove what any one of those people said or achieved. They show something simpler: at the meeting meant to limit an industry, that industry is among the largest groups present, and in two recent years the person chairing the meeting came from it. For a child, the closest comparison is a class deciding the rules for a game while the team that benefits from the old rules supplies the referee.",
  bridge="The slides follow three climate summits in order. The first two were hosted by oil-producing states and chaired by men from their state oil companies. The third was held at the edge of the Amazon. The last slide moves to the plastics treaty talks from the previous chapter, where the same count was made.",
  rec="Fossil fuel lobbyists outnumbered almost every national delegation at three climate summits in a row, and two of those summits were chaired by oil company men.",
  out="The industries being regulated attend the talks in larger numbers than most countries, and sometimes run them. But a treaty still has to be turned into national rules. Before following it down to the ministry, one more body at the top needs explaining: a court where a company can sue a country for making those rules.",
  roles=["Where the talks were held", "Where the chairing company is based"],
  steps=[
   S("2023", "Dubai", "Dubai: The Oil Company's Chief in the Chair", "The 2023 climate summit, COP28, was held in Dubai in the United Arab Emirates. The host country chooses the president, who chairs the talks and steers the final text. The Emirates chose Sultan Al Jaber, who was at the same time chief executive of ADNOC, the state oil company of Abu Dhabi, a post he has held since 2016. The coalition Kick Big Polluters Out counted 2,456 fossil fuel lobbyists registered at the summit, the most ever recorded and several times the previous year's figure. This matters because the president decides which draft goes to the floor and when. In Dubai the person holding that power also ran one of the world's largest oil producers.", "https://kickbigpollutersout.org/",
     add={"United Arab Emirates": 0}, arcs=[("Abu Dhabi", "Dubai")], pins=[("Dubai", "COP28: 2,456 lobbyists"), ("Abu Dhabi", "ADNOC")], pl=["adnoc", "unfccc"], ex=["kbpo"], z=3.4),
   S("2024", "Baku", "Baku: The Same Pattern a Year Later", "The following summit, COP29, was held in Baku, the capital of Azerbaijan, a country whose exports are mostly oil and gas. Its president was Mukhtar Babayev, Azerbaijan's environment minister, who had worked at SOCAR, the state oil company, from 1994 to 2018. The same coalition counted 1,773 fossil fuel lobbyists. That was fewer than in Dubai, but still more than the delegations of almost every country. Two summits in a row had now been chaired by men who built their careers inside state oil companies. The point is not that either man broke a rule. It is that no rule prevented it.", "https://kickbigpollutersout.org/",
     add={"Azerbaijan": 0}, pins=[("Baku", "COP29: 1,773 lobbyists; SOCAR")], pl=["socar", "unfccc"], ex=["kbpo"], z=3.4),
   S("2025", "Belém", "Belém: One Person in Every 25", "COP30 was held in Belém, a Brazilian city at the mouth of the Amazon. The count there was 1,602 fossil fuel lobbyists. Fewer people attended overall, so the industry's share rose to about one in every 25 people present, the highest share yet recorded. Only one delegation was larger: the host country's, with 3,805 people. To picture it, imagine a school assembly of 500 pupils in which 20 are there on behalf of one company. Many came on the badges of business groups such as the International Chamber of Commerce and the International Emissions Trading Association, which is how company staff enter as observers. Over five years the coalition has counted about 7,000 such registrations.", "https://kickbigpollutersout.org/",
     add={"Brazil": 0}, arcs=[("Paris", "Belém"), ("Geneva", "Belém")], pins=[("Belém", "COP30: 1,602 lobbyists"), ("Paris", "International Chamber of Commerce"), ("Geneva", "Emissions Trading Association")], pl=["icc", "ieta", "unfccc"], ex=["kbpo"]),
   S("2025", "Geneva", "The Same Count at the Plastics Talks", "The pattern is not limited to climate. At the plastics treaty session in Geneva in August 2025, the Center for International Environmental Law counted at least 234 lobbyists from the fossil fuel and chemical industries. All 27 countries of the European Union, plus the Union's own officials, sent 233 between them. Some lobbyists sat inside national delegations, where they can enter rooms closed to observers. Earlier sessions had 143, 196 and 221, so the number rose each time as the talks moved closer to a decision. As the last chapter showed, the session ended with no treaty at all.", "https://www.ciel.org/",
     add={"Switzerland": 0}, pins=[("Geneva", "234 industry lobbyists; EU delegations, 233")], pl=["ciel", "acc", "unep"], ex=["plastics234"], z=3.4),
  ],
  src=[{"t": "Kick Big Polluters Out, COP30 analysis (November 2025)", "u": "https://kickbigpollutersout.org/"}])

F(id="isds", part=0, title="The Court That Can Overrule a Ministry",
  kick="Investment treaties let a company sue a country for passing an environmental rule, and collect.",
  sum="Environmental treaties, as the last chapters showed, mostly have no penalties. Another family of treaties does. Since the 1960s governments have signed about three thousand investment treaties with one another. Each promises fair treatment to the other country's investors. Most of them also give those investors a special right: if a company believes a government has treated it unfairly, it can skip that country's own courts and take the government to a panel of three private arbitrators. This is called investor-state dispute settlement, or ISDS.<br><br>The panel cannot cancel a law. It can order the country to pay, and the sums are often based on profits the company says it would have made in the future. An award can be collected against the country's property abroad. Only investors can bring a case. A community harmed by a mine cannot use the system against the company.<br><br>This is where the two kinds of treaty meet. When a ministry refuses a permit, tightens a limit or bans drilling, it may be acting on an environmental promise that carries no penalty, against an investment promise that does. A government adding up the cost of a new rule has to include the chance of being sued for it. Lawyers call this effect regulatory chill.",
  bridge="The slides begin at the arbitration centre itself, inside the World Bank. Three cases follow, each a different kind of environmental decision: a refused pipeline, a permit condition and a drilling ban. The last slide shows the countries that have decided to leave the treaty used in two of those cases.",
  rec="A company can sue a country before private arbitrators for refusing a permit or banning drilling, and be paid for profits it expected; a polluted community has no such court.",
  out="At the top of the chain, then, the environmental promises are voluntary, the industries are in the room, and the one set of promises with real penalties protects investors. With that in view, the story moves down a level, to the national government and what it does with its environmental laws.",
  roles=["Where the claim was heard or the treaty is based", "The country that was sued", "Where the company is based"],
  steps=[
   S("1966", "Washington", "A Court Inside the World Bank", "The largest forum for these cases is the International Centre for Settlement of Investment Disputes, known as ICSID. It was created in 1966 and sits inside the World Bank in Washington, the same institution that chapter 1 showed holding the environmental funds. Each case is decided by three arbitrators, usually private lawyers, one chosen by each side and one agreed between them. There is no appeal on the merits. More cases have been brought by investors in oil, gas and mining than by any other kind of business. The Energy Charter Treaty, whose office is in Brussels, is the single agreement used most often, and it covers only energy.", "https://icsid.worldbank.org/",
     add={"United States": 0, "Belgium": 0}, arcs=[("Brussels", "Washington")], pins=[("Washington", "ICSID, at the World Bank"), ("Brussels", "Energy Charter Treaty office")], pl=["icsid", "worldbank", "ect", "pca"]),
   S("2016", "Washington", "A Refused Pipeline: 15 Billion Dollars Claimed", "Regional Example: The Keystone XL pipeline was planned to carry oil from the Canadian province of Alberta to the United States. In 2015 President Obama refused it a permit, giving climate as a reason. In January 2016 the Canadian company behind it announced a claim against the United States for 15 billion dollars under the North American Free Trade Agreement. The claim was suspended when the next president approved the pipeline. When the permit was cancelled again in 2021, the company filed a second claim, which a tribunal dismissed in 2024, ruling it had no power to hear it. The government was not made to pay. It did have to defend the same decision twice, at a claimed price larger than its environment agency's yearly budget.", "https://icsid.worldbank.org/cases/case-database",
     add={"United States": 1, "Canada": 2}, arcs=[("Calgary", "Washington"), ("Hardisty", "Houston")], pins=[("Calgary", "TC Energy"), ("Hardisty", "Pipeline start")], pl=["tc", "icsid"], ex=["keystone"], reg=True),
   S("2009 to 2011", "Hamburg", "A Permit Condition: Loosened to Settle", "Regional Example: A case does not have to be won to change a rule. The Swedish state power company Vattenfall built a coal power plant at Moorburg, on the River Elbe in Hamburg. The city attached strict conditions to the water permit, limiting how much river water the plant could take and how warm it could return it. In 2009 Vattenfall sued Germany for 1.4 billion euros under the Energy Charter Treaty. The case ended in a settlement in 2011. As part of it, the plant received a new permit with weaker conditions. This is the plainest example on the map of the chain reaching down: a treaty signed by a national government was used to rewrite a permit issued by a city.", "https://icsid.worldbank.org/cases/case-database",
     add={"Germany": 1, "Sweden": 2}, arcs=[("Stockholm", "Moorburg")], pins=[("Moorburg", "Moorburg coal plant"), ("Stockholm", "Vattenfall")], pl=["vattenfall", "bmuv"], ex=["moorburg"], reg=True, z=3.6),
   S("2022", "Ortona", "A Drilling Ban: Paid For After Leaving", "Regional Example: After protests along the Adriatic coast, Italy banned new oil and gas drilling within 12 nautical miles of its shore. That ended a planned offshore field called Ombrina Mare. Its owner, the British company Rockhopper, sued under the Energy Charter Treaty. Italy had left that treaty in 2016, but the treaty contains what is known as a sunset clause: investors may still sue for 20 years after a country leaves. In 2022 the tribunal ordered Italy to pay about 190 million euros plus interest, a figure built on the profits the field was expected to earn. Italy challenged the award. The company had spent a fraction of that sum on the project.", "https://icsid.worldbank.org/cases/case-database",
     add={"Italy": 1, "United Kingdom": 2}, arcs=[("London", "Ortona")], pins=[("Ortona", "Ombrina Mare field")], pl=["rockhopper", "ect"], ex=["rockhopper_x"], reg=True, z=3.4),
   S("2016 to 2025", "Brussels", "Countries Begin to Leave", "Cases like these led governments to walk away from the treaty. Italy left in 2016. France, Germany and Poland left in 2023, and Luxembourg in 2024. Portugal, Spain, the United Kingdom, the Netherlands and Denmark followed in 2025, and the European Union itself withdrew on 28 June 2025. Several of them said openly that the treaty could not be squared with their climate laws. The sunset clause means investors can still bring claims against each of them for two decades after their exit, so the cost of having signed outlasts the decision to leave. The last part of the story returns to this as one of the places where the chain was pushed back.", "https://www.energycharter.org/",
     add={"Belgium": 0, "Italy": 1, "France": 1, "Germany": 1, "Poland": 1, "Luxembourg": 1, "Portugal": 1, "Spain": 1, "United Kingdom": 1, "Netherlands": 1, "Denmark": 1}, pins=[("Brussels", "EU withdraws, June 2025")], pl=["ect", "eu_env"], z=3.2),
  ],
  src=[{"t": "UNCTAD Investment Dispute Settlement Navigator", "u": "https://investmentpolicy.unctad.org/investment-dispute-settlement"}])

F(id="licence", part=1, title="A Law That Hands Out Permits",
  kick="Most environmental law does not forbid pollution. It sets an amount and licenses it.",
  sum="The story now moves from the international level to the national government. A common picture of environmental law is a list of things that are forbidden. Most of it is not built that way. The typical environmental law creates a system of permits. A company applies to release a stated amount of a substance into the air or a river, or to clear a stated area of land, and an agency grants permission, usually with conditions.<br><br>Seen this way, a permit is permission to cause a measured amount of harm. The harm that results is then lawful. A person who lives beside the site and becomes ill cannot complain that the law was broken, because it was followed.<br><br>The same design applies to new projects. Before a large mine, dam or plant is approved, most countries require a study of the damage it is expected to cause, called an environmental impact assessment. In most systems the company proposing the project hires and pays the consultants who write it. The study is a step on the way to a permit. It is rarely a reason for refusing one.<br><br>There is a further reason governments issue these permits. Many of them earn a large part of their income from the industries concerned.",
  bridge="The first slide explains where the permit model and the impact study came from. The second visits one place where every plant holds a valid permit and the result can be measured in the air. The third is a map of how much of each country's income comes from oil, gas, coal, minerals and timber.",
  rec="Environmental law mostly licenses pollution in set amounts, on studies paid for by the applicant, and many governments earn their living from the industries they license.",
  out="A permit system makes harm lawful, and a government that lives on resource income has reasons to keep the permits coming. The next chapter adds a second way the state stands behind the same industries: with public money.",
  roles=["Where the model began or is applied"],
  steps=[
   S("1970", "Washington", "The Impact Study, Paid For by the Applicant", "The modern model began in the United States. A law that took effect in 1970, the National Environmental Policy Act, required federal agencies to study the environmental effects of major projects before approving them. Nearly every country has since adopted some version of this environmental impact assessment. The study describes the expected damage and proposes ways to reduce it. In most countries the developer selects and pays the consultancy that writes it, and a whole industry now exists to do that work. The law generally requires that the effects be considered. It does not require the agency to say no if they are bad. This is why later slides can show sites that passed every study and still harmed the people around them.", "https://www.epa.gov/nepa/what-national-environmental-policy-act",
     add={"United States": 0}, pins=[("Washington", "National Environmental Policy Act, 1970")], pl=["epa_us", "erm"]),
   S("2021", "St James Parish", "Louisiana: Every Plant Has a Permit", "Regional Example: Along the Mississippi River between Baton Rouge and New Orleans stand several hundred industrial facilities, many of them petrochemical plants that turn oil and gas into plastics and chemicals. Each one operates under air and water permits issued by the state's environmental agency. Residents call the area Cancer Alley. Analyses of the federal government's own air data have found parts of it where the estimated risk of cancer from industrial air pollution is 47 times the level the Environmental Protection Agency treats as acceptable. Most of the people living closest are Black and poor. Nothing here depends on a company breaking the law. It is what the permits, added together, allow.", "https://www.ohchr.org/en/press-releases/2021/03/usa-environmental-racism-cancer-alley-must-end-experts",
     add={"United States": 0}, arcs=[("Baton Rouge", "St James Parish")], pins=[("St James Parish", "Cancer Alley"), ("Baton Rouge", "State permit agency")], pl=["ldeq", "epa_us"], reg=True, z=3.8),
   S("Latest figures", "Every country with figures", "Where the State Lives on What It Licenses", "This map shows one reason permits are granted. The World Bank measures natural resource rents: the income a country earns from oil, gas, coal, minerals and forests above the cost of extracting them, as a share of everything the country produces. In Libya, Iraq, the Democratic Republic of the Congo, the Republic of the Congo and Zambia that share is more than a third. Where it is high, the treasury depends on the same wells and mines that the environment ministry regulates, and state companies are often the operators. An environment minister in such a country is asking colleagues to limit the source of their budget. Countries in the palest band rely far less on extraction at home, though many import what others extract.", "https://data.worldbank.org/indicator/NY.GDP.TOTL.RT.ZS",
     data={"k": "rents", "only": None}, z=1.15, w=1),
  ],
  src=[{"t": "UN Special Rapporteur on human rights and the environment, report on sacrifice zones, A/HRC/49/53 (2022)", "u": "https://www.ohchr.org/en/documents/thematic-reports/ahrc4953-right-clean-healthy-and-sustainable-environment-non-toxic"}])

F(id="subsidy", part=1, title="The State Pays for What Its Laws Restrict",
  kick="Governments spend far more supporting fossil fuels and intensive farming than on protecting the environment from them.",
  sum="A government speaks through its laws, and it also speaks through its budget. The last chapter showed that environmental law licenses harm in set amounts. This chapter looks at the money the same governments spend on the activities being licensed.<br><br>A subsidy is public support that makes something cheaper to produce or to buy than it would otherwise be. Some subsidies are direct: a government holds the price of petrol below what it costs to supply, or pays farmers according to how much they produce. Others are indirect. When fuel is sold at a price that leaves out the cost of the illness and warming it causes, the public pays that cost instead, in hospital bills and damage.<br><br>The sums are not close to those in the environment budget. They are hundreds of times larger. This is why the chain of command in chapter 1 can contain an environment ministry and an environmental fund and still produce rising pollution: the same government is paying, through a different ministry, for more of what the environment ministry is asked to limit. For the companies involved, a subsidy is income and an environmental rule is a cost, and they lobby for the first and against the second.",
  bridge="The first slide gives the worldwide figure for fossil fuels and sets it beside the main environmental fund. The second maps direct fossil fuel subsidies country by country. The third turns to farming.",
  rec="Governments support fossil fuels with 7 trillion dollars a year and pay most farm support to the most damaging kinds of farming, while the main environmental fund receives 5.3 billion dollars in four years.",
  out="The state licenses the harm and helps pay for it. The next chapter shows what happens to the rules that remain when an industry asks for them to be removed.",
  roles=["Where the figures are counted"],
  steps=[
   S("2022", "Washington", "Seven Trillion Dollars in One Year", "The International Monetary Fund lends to governments and studies their budgets. Its researchers counted fossil fuel subsidies of 7 trillion dollars in 2022, or 7.1 per cent of the world's income. Of that, 1.3 trillion was direct support that kept fuel prices low. The rest was the cost of pollution and warming that the price of fuel leaves for the public to pay. To make the figure picturable: 7 trillion dollars a year is about 19 billion dollars a day. The Global Environment Facility from chapter 1 was promised 5.3 billion dollars for four years. Fossil fuel support reaches that amount in under seven hours. Both sums come from the same group of governments.", "https://www.imf.org/en/Publications/WP/Issues/2023/08/22/IMF-Fossil-Fuel-Subsidies-Data-2023-Update-537281",
     add={"United States": 0}, pins=[("Washington", "IMF count: $7 trillion; GEF: $5.3 billion over four years")], pl=["imf", "gef"], ex=["imf7"]),
   S("Latest figures", "Every country with figures", "Direct Fossil Fuel Subsidies, Country by Country", "This map uses a narrower measure than the last slide. It counts only direct subsidies for producing and using fossil fuels, as governments report them for the United Nations' development goals, shown as a share of each country's income. The highest figures are in countries that produce oil and gas and sell it cheaply at home: Iran at about 28 per cent, then Turkmenistan, Uzbekistan, Algeria and Libya at between 12 and 14 per cent. In those countries the subsidy is larger than most ministries' entire budgets. Many countries that subsidise fuel this way have also signed the Paris Agreement, which shows how the voluntary pledges of chapter 2 and the national budget can point in opposite directions.", "https://ourworldindata.org/grapher/fossil-fuel-subsidies-gdp",
     data={"k": "subs_gdp", "only": None}, z=1.15, w=1),
   S("2021", "Rome", "Farm Support: 87 Per Cent Harmful", "Farming is the main cause of forest loss and a major source of water pollution, and it is regulated by environmental law in most countries. It is also supported with public money. In 2021 three United Nations agencies, led from the Food and Agriculture Organization in Rome, reported that governments give farmers about 540 billion dollars a year. They found that 87 per cent of this support either distorts prices or harms nature and health, for example by paying more to those who produce more beef, milk or rice, or by subsidising fertiliser and pesticides. So in farming too, the larger part of public spending pushes in the opposite direction from the environmental rules on the same land.", "https://www.fao.org/documents/card/en/c/cb6562en",
     add={"Italy": 0}, pins=[("Rome", "FAO: $540 billion a year of farm support")], pl=["fao", "unep", "jbs", "cargill"], ex=["farm540"]),
  ],
  src=[{"t": "IMF, Fossil Fuel Subsidies", "u": "https://www.imf.org/en/Topics/climate-change/energy-subsidies"}])

F(id="rollback", part=1, title="Rules Removed on Request",
  kick="When an environmental rule begins to cost an industry money, governments on every continent have taken it away.",
  sum="A permit system and a subsidy still leave some rules that bite: a limit on what may be discharged into a river, a requirement to study a project before building it, a ban on clearing a forest. This chapter looks at how long such rules last.<br><br>Environmental law is unusually easy to undo. Much of it is written as regulations, which a government can rewrite without a vote in the legislature. Much of the rest depends on budgets and on how an agency chooses to interpret its powers. A new government, or the same government under pressure, can remove in months what took years to build. The change is often placed inside a larger bill about the budget or jobs, where it receives little debate.<br><br>The pattern across countries is consistent enough to describe simply. An industry or its association states which rules stand in its way. The government describes those rules as red tape, or as a burden on competitiveness, and promises simplification. The rules are then narrowed, delayed or repealed. The examples that follow come from North America, South America, Southeast Asia and Europe, and from governments of different political colours.",
  bridge="The slides move from the largest economy outwards. The first is the United States, where the legal foundation of climate rules was removed in 2026. The second is Canada, where an industry letter listed the laws to change. Brazil and Indonesia follow, and the last slide shows the European Union narrowing one of its own newest laws before it applied.",
  rec="The United States, Canada, Brazil, Indonesia and the European Union each removed or narrowed environmental rules after industry asked, often inside budget or jobs bills.",
  out="Rules that cost money are removed on request. The ones that survive still have to be enforced by someone, and the next chapter looks at who that someone is and how often they arrive.",
  roles=["Where rules were removed"],
  steps=[
   S("2017 to 2026", "Washington", "United States: The Foundation Removed", "Regional Example: Between 2017 and 2021 the United States government reversed or began reversing more than 100 environmental rules, by the count kept by the New York Times. Many were restored after 2021. The second round went deeper. In 2009 the Environmental Protection Agency had formally found that greenhouse gases endanger public health, which gave it the legal duty to limit them. On 18 February 2026 the agency published a rule cancelling that finding and repealing every federal greenhouse gas limit on cars and trucks. Its own fact sheet called it the single largest deregulatory action in the country's history. Because other climate rules rest on the same finding, removing it weakens them all at once.", "https://www.epa.gov/regulations-emissions-vehicles-and-engines/final-rule-rescission-greenhouse-gas-endangerment-finding",
     add={"United States": 0}, pins=[("Washington", "Endangerment finding cancelled, February 2026")], pl=["epa_us", "api", "uschamber"], ex=["endanger"], reg=True),
   S("2011 to 2012", "Ottawa", "Canada: The Letter and the Budget Bill", "Regional Example: In December 2011, four Canadian oil and gas associations wrote jointly to two federal ministers. Their letter named the laws they wanted changed, including the acts on environmental assessment, fisheries, species at risk and the National Energy Board. A few months later the government introduced its 2012 budget bill, more than 400 pages long. It replaced the environmental assessment act with a narrower one and amended the others named in the letter. The connection became public in 2013, when the letter was released under the access to information law. The significance is the sequence: the regulated industries supplied the list, and the changes arrived inside a bill about the budget.", "https://www.cbc.ca/news/politics/energy-industry-letter-suggested-environmental-law-changes-1.1346258",
     add={"Canada": 0}, arcs=[("Calgary", "Ottawa")], pins=[("Ottawa", "Budget bill C-38, 2012"), ("Calgary", "Industry associations")], pl=["capp", "eccc"], ex=["c38"], reg=True),
   S("2020", "Brasília", "Brazil: While the Press Looked Elsewhere", "Regional Example: In April 2020, during the first wave of Covid-19, Brazil's cabinet met and was filmed. The Supreme Court later ordered the video released. In it the environment minister, Ricardo Salles, told his colleagues that the pandemic was an opportunity: with the press focused on the virus, the government should push through changes to environmental rules by ministerial order, which needs no vote in Congress. During that government, deforestation in the Brazilian Amazon rose to its highest level in more than a decade. The recording matters because it is rare to hear the method described by the person using it.", "https://www.reuters.com/article/us-brazil-politics-environment-idUSKBN22Y30Y",
     add={"Brazil": 0}, pins=[("Brasília", "Cabinet meeting, 22 April 2020")], pl=["mma_br", "ibama", "jbs"], ex=["boiada"], reg=True),
   S("2020", "Jakarta", "Indonesia: One Law to Change Dozens", "Regional Example: In October 2020 Indonesia's parliament passed the Omnibus Law on Job Creation, a single law that amended about 80 existing ones in order to attract investment. Among its changes, it narrowed which projects need a full environmental impact assessment, reduced the public's part in reviewing those assessments, and moved permit powers from regions to the central government. Indonesia holds the third-largest area of tropical rainforest in the world and is the largest producer of palm oil and of nickel. In 2021 the Constitutional Court found that the law had been passed improperly and ordered it repaired. The government reissued it by emergency regulation.", "https://www.reuters.com/article/us-indonesia-economy-law-environment-idUSKBN26Q0F6",
     add={"Indonesia": 0}, pins=[("Jakarta", "Omnibus Law, 2020")], pl=["klhk"], reg=True, z=3.0),
   S("2025 to 2026", "Brussels", "European Union: Narrowed Before It Applied", "Regional Example: In 2024 the European Union passed a law requiring large companies to find and address environmental damage and human rights abuses in their supply chains, including outside Europe. In 2025, after lobbying by business federations and several governments, the European Commission proposed a package called Omnibus I. The final version, approved on 24 February 2026, restricts the law to companies with more than 5,000 employees and 1.5 billion euros in sales and delays it to 2029. Advisers estimated that this removes most of the companies originally covered. A separate law against importing goods grown on deforested land was delayed for a second time, to the end of 2026.", "https://www.consilium.europa.eu/en/policies/simplification-of-eu-legislation/",
     add={"Belgium": 0}, pins=[("Brussels", "Omnibus I approved, February 2026")], pl=["eu_env", "businesseurope", "cefic"], ex=["omnibus_eu"], reg=True, z=3.2),
  ],
  src=[{"t": "Harvard Environmental and Energy Law Program, regulatory tracker", "u": "https://eelp.law.harvard.edu/"}])

F(id="enforce", part=1, title="The Inspector Who Seldom Comes",
  kick="Where a rule survives, the penalty for breaking it is usually smaller than the profit from doing so.",
  sum="Suppose a rule has survived everything described so far. It is written down, it has not been repealed, and a site is breaking it. Someone now has to notice, prove it and impose a penalty. This is enforcement, and the United Nations' 2019 review found it to be the weakest link everywhere it looked. Its conclusion was that the growth in environmental laws had not been matched by the means to apply them.<br><br>Three features recur. First, agencies have too few inspectors for the number of sites, and their budgets are set by governments that, as the last chapter showed, may want less enforcement. Second, many systems rely on the company to measure its own pollution and report the results. Third, when a penalty does arrive it is usually a fine, and a fine is a price. A company can compare it with the money saved by not complying. If the fine is smaller, breaking the rule is the sensible business decision, and the fine becomes a cost of doing business. In some countries part of it can even be deducted from tax.<br><br>The reason this belongs in a story about profit is that weak enforcement is worth money to those who are regulated. Every inspection that does not happen is a saving.",
  bridge="The first slide gives the scale of environmental law-breaking worldwide. The next two look at the largest penalties ever imposed, on an oil company and a car maker, to see what even a record fine amounts to. The last slide is a country where the regulated companies do the monitoring themselves.",
  rec="Environmental crime is worth up to 258 billion dollars a year; the largest fines ever imposed were partly tax-deductible or followed years of undetected cheating; and in England polluters count their own spills.",
  out="That completes the picture at the level of the state: it licenses harm, subsidises it, removes rules on request and rarely enforces the rest. The next part asks where the words in the laws and standards come from in the first place.",
  roles=["Where the offence or the enforcer is"],
  steps=[
   S("2016", "Lyon", "A Criminal Business Worth Up to 258 Billion Dollars", "INTERPOL is the organisation through which national police forces cooperate, and its headquarters are in Lyon, France. In 2016, together with UNEP, it estimated the yearly value of environmental crime at between 91 and 258 billion dollars. That covers illegal logging, fishing and mining, the trade in protected wildlife and the dumping of waste. The estimate was 26 per cent higher than two years earlier, and larger than the illegal trade in small arms. More than half comes from illegal logging and forest clearing. The figure shows how much money is made by ignoring environmental law outright. The following slides look at the companies that operate inside the law and are caught breaking it.", "https://www.unep.org/resources/report/rise-environmental-crime-growing-threat-natural-resources-peace-development-and",
     add={"France": 0, "Kenya": 0}, arcs=[("Lyon", "Nairobi")], pins=[("Lyon", "INTERPOL")], pl=["interpol", "unep"], ex=["crime258"]),
   S("2010 to 2015", "Gulf of Mexico", "The Largest Penalty, Partly Paid by Taxpayers", "Regional Example: In April 2010 the Deepwater Horizon drilling rig, working for BP, exploded in the Gulf of Mexico. Eleven workers died and oil flowed for 87 days. In 2015 BP agreed to pay 20.8 billion dollars to the United States government and five states, still the largest environmental settlement in the country's history. Under United States tax rules a fine paid to the government cannot be deducted, but compensation and clean-up payments can. Only 5.5 billion dollars of the settlement was labelled a penalty. The consumer group US PIRG calculated that the other 15.3 billion could be deducted, which shifts part of the cost to the public. BP remained one of the largest oil companies in the world.", "https://www.justice.gov/opa/pr/us-and-five-gulf-states-reach-historic-settlement-bp-resolve-civil-lawsuit-over-deepwater",
     add={"United States": 0}, arcs=[("London", "Macondo well")], pins=[("Macondo well", "Deepwater Horizon, 2010"), ("London", "BP")], pl=["bp", "epa_us"], ex=["dwh"], reg=True, z=3.0),
   S("2015", "Wolfsburg", "The Test That Was Told What It Wanted to Hear", "Regional Example: Car exhaust limits are among the strictest environmental rules there are. In 2015 the Environmental Protection Agency announced that Volkswagen had fitted diesel cars with software that recognised when the car was being tested and lowered its pollution only then. On the road, the cars emitted many times the legal limit. About 11 million cars worldwide carried the software, and it had gone undetected by regulators for years; it was found by university researchers testing cars on the road. Volkswagen has since paid 33.3 billion dollars. The case shows the weakness of rules that are checked in a laboratory by a method the company knows in advance.", "https://www.epa.gov/vw/learn-about-volkswagen-violations",
     add={"Germany": 0, "United States": 0}, arcs=[("Wolfsburg", "Washington")], pins=[("Wolfsburg", "Volkswagen")], pl=["vw", "epa_us"], ex=["vw_x"], reg=True),
   S("2009 to 2025", "Bristol", "England: The Polluter Holds the Meter", "Regional Example: England's water and sewage system was sold to private companies in 1989. Since 2009 those companies have been responsible for monitoring their own discharges and reporting the results to the Environment Agency, an arrangement known as operator self-monitoring. Their monitors recorded about 3.61 million hours of sewage flowing from storm overflows into rivers and the sea in 2024, the highest total on record. In 2025 the largest company, Thames Water, received a record fine from the water regulator. For years before the monitors were fitted, nobody had a count at all. A regulator that depends on the regulated company for its facts can only act on what the company tells it.", "https://www.gov.uk/government/news/environment-agency-publishes-storm-overflow-spill-data-for-2024",
     add={"United Kingdom": 0}, arcs=[("Reading", "Bristol")], pins=[("Bristol", "Environment Agency"), ("Reading", "Thames Water")], pl=["ea_uk", "thames"], ex=["sewage"], reg=True, z=3.8),
  ],
  src=[{"t": "UNEP, Environmental Rule of Law: First Global Report (2019)", "u": UNEP19}])

F(id="authors", part=2, title="Bills Drafted by the Regulated",
  kick="The text of a law, or of the official study behind it, has repeatedly been traced to the industry it covers.",
  sum="The first two parts followed the chain from international bodies down to national governments. This part asks a narrower question: who actually writes the words?<br><br>A law is drafted by someone before a legislature votes on it, and a regulation rests on technical studies that someone prepares. Lawmakers are few and have small staffs. Companies and their trade associations have lawyers and scientists whose full-time work is the subject at hand. The association offers to help, and its draft becomes the starting point. Sometimes it becomes the finished text.<br><br>This is seldom illegal. In many countries it is the ordinary way laws are made, and it happens in private. It becomes visible only by accident: when identical wording appears in many places at once, when a computer file carries a record of where it was created, or when someone compares an official report with a company's application line by line and finds them the same.<br><br>The consequence for the reader is that an environmental law cannot be assumed to say what the public needed. It may say what the regulated industry was willing to accept, or what it wrote.",
  bridge="Three cases follow, each caught in a different way. In the first, a model bill was copied across state legislatures. In the second, a file's hidden properties named its origin. In the third, a scientific study compared two documents and measured how much of one had been copied from the other.",
  rec="A protest law copied across 17 states, a chemical safety bill traced to the chemical lobby's computer, and a pesticide review half-copied from the maker's application show who drafts the text.",
  out="When industries draft the law, the law says what they can live with. But persuading lawmakers and the public to accept such drafts takes a longer effort, and the next chapter follows the best-documented one.",
  roles=["Where the text was written or adopted"],
  steps=[
   S("2017 to 2021", "Arlington", "One Model Bill, Seventeen States", "Regional Example: The American Legislative Exchange Council, known as ALEC, is funded largely by companies. At its meetings, state lawmakers and company representatives sit on committees together and vote on model bills, which are ready-made laws with blanks for the state's name. In 2016 thousands of people joined the Standing Rock Sioux in North Dakota to oppose the Dakota Access oil pipeline. Soon afterwards ALEC adopted a model bill, based on a new Oklahoma law, that sharply raised penalties for trespassing near pipelines and for organisations that support such protest. By 2021 at least 17 states had passed laws of this kind. A person arrested under one of them is facing a text approved in a room that included the pipeline industry.", "https://www.icnl.org/usprotestlawtracker/",
     add={"United States": 0}, arcs=[("Oklahoma City", "Arlington"), ("Arlington", "Mandan")], pins=[("Arlington", "ALEC"), ("Oklahoma City", "The Oklahoma law, 2017"), ("Mandan", "Standing Rock protests")], pl=["alec", "et", "api"], ex=["alec_cip"], reg=True, z=3.0),
   S("2015", "Washington", "A File That Named Its Author", "Regional Example: The main United States law on industrial chemicals dates from 1976 and was widely agreed to have failed: under it, the EPA had been unable even to ban asbestos. In 2015 the Senate took up a bill to rewrite it. When a draft was sent round as a computer file, reporters opened its properties, the hidden record of where a file was made. The record named the American Chemistry Council, the trade association of the chemical makers the law would regulate. The council said it had not written the bill and that the file had passed through many hands. A revised law passed in 2016. The episode is useful because such a trace is usually wiped before a draft is shared.", "https://www.sfgate.com/politics/article/Questions-raised-on-authorship-of-chemicals-bill-6137823.php",
     add={"United States": 0}, pins=[("Washington", "Senate chemicals bill, 2015")], pl=["acc", "dow", "epa_us"], ex=["tsca"], reg=True),
   S("2017 to 2019", "Berlin", "A Safety Review Half Copied From the Maker", "Regional Example: Glyphosate is the most widely used weedkiller in the world. In 2017 the European Union renewed its approval, relying on a safety assessment prepared by Germany's federal risk institute in Berlin. Members of the European Parliament then commissioned two specialists to compare that assessment with the application submitted by the manufacturers, led by Monsanto. They reported in 2019 that half of the chapters reviewing published health studies were plagiarised from the application, including the companies' own judgment of each study, presented as the authority's. The institute said that adopting text from applicants after checking it was normal practice. That reply is the point: the practice is normal.", "https://www.europarl.europa.eu/news/en/press-room/20190115IPR23551/eu-glyphosate-approval-was-based-on-plagiarised-monsanto-text-report-finds",
     add={"Germany": 0, "Belgium": 0}, arcs=[("Leverkusen", "Berlin"), ("Berlin", "Brussels")], pins=[("Berlin", "Federal risk institute"), ("Leverkusen", "Bayer, owner of Monsanto"), ("Brussels", "EU approval, 2017")], pl=["bayer", "croplife", "eu_env", "syngenta"], ex=["glyphosate"], reg=True, z=3.4),
  ],
  src=[{"t": "International Center for Not-for-Profit Law, US Protest Law Tracker", "u": "https://www.icnl.org/usprotestlawtracker/"}])

F(id="lobby", part=2, title="What Was Known, and What Was Said",
  kick="Oil companies understood climate change in the 1970s and paid for decades to keep the law from acting on it.",
  sum="The last chapter showed industries supplying the text of laws. A law also needs a public that accepts it, or at least does not demand something stronger. This chapter follows the longest and best-documented effort to shape what the public and lawmakers believed.<br><br>It concerns climate change, because that is where the internal company papers have become public through court cases and journalists. The same methods were used earlier for tobacco, lead and asbestos, and later for plastics.<br><br>The method has three parts. First, a company learns from its own scientists that its product causes harm. Second, it says something different in public: that the science is uncertain and more study is needed. Third, it pays trade associations and other groups to repeat that message and to lobby against rules, so the argument does not appear to come from the company itself.<br><br>The aim was never to win the scientific argument. It was to delay the law, because each year without a rule is a year of sales. This is the clearest example on the map of how profit and the failure of environmental law are joined: the delay was bought, and it was cheap compared with what it protected.",
  bridge="The slides run in order of time. The first is what one company knew in 1977. The second is the plan written twenty years later to cast doubt on that knowledge. The third counts what the largest companies were still spending after the Paris Agreement. The fourth shows the same method applied to plastic.",
  rec="Exxon's scientists confirmed the warming in 1977; the industry's association planned in 1998 to spread doubt; five companies still spend about 200 million dollars a year lobbying on climate.",
  out="Doubt was manufactured to delay the law. The next chapter looks at a more direct route into government: the people from industry who are appointed to run the agencies.",
  roles=["Where the knowledge or the campaign was based"],
  steps=[
   S("1977", "Houston", "1977: The Company's Own Scientists", "In July 1977 a senior scientist at Exxon, James Black, briefed the company's management. He told them that scientists generally agreed the most likely way humanity was changing the global climate was by burning fossil fuels. Exxon then funded its own research from 1979 to 1982, including measurements taken from one of its oil tankers. Its scientists' projections of future warming have since been compared with what happened and found to be accurate. The date is what matters. It was eleven years before the subject reached the United States Congress in a well-known 1988 hearing, and fifteen years before the first climate treaty. The company knew early and knew well.", "https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.1088/1748-9326/aa815f",
     add={"United States": 0}, pins=[("Houston", "Exxon, 1977")], pl=["exxon"], ex=["exxon77"]),
   S("1998", "Washington", "1998: A Plan to Spread Doubt", "After the Kyoto Protocol was agreed in 1997, a small group met at the Washington offices of the American Petroleum Institute, the oil industry's trade association. It included staff from oil companies and from organisations they funded. The group wrote a plan, later leaked to the press, for a campaign aimed at the public, the media and schools. The plan defined success: it would be achieved when average citizens and the media came to see uncertainty as part of what everybody knew about climate science. Researchers at Harvard later measured the gap. About four-fifths of Exxon's internal and scientific papers accepted that people were causing climate change. Four-fifths of its paid newspaper columns expressed doubt.", "https://www.climatefiles.com/trade-group/american-petroleum-institute/1998-global-climate-science-communications-team-action-plan/",
     add={"United States": 0}, arcs=[("Houston", "Washington")], pins=[("Washington", "American Petroleum Institute")], pl=["api", "exxon", "chevron", "uschamber"], ex=["exxon77"]),
   S("2019", "London", "After Paris: 200 Million Dollars a Year", "The effort did not end when governments signed the Paris Agreement. The research group InfluenceMap examined the five largest oil and gas companies listed on stock markets: ExxonMobil, Shell, Chevron, BP and Total. It estimated in 2019 that together they spent about 200 million dollars a year on lobbying to control, delay or block climate policy, much of it through trade associations, and more than a billion dollars over three years on lobbying and climate-related advertising combined. Set that against chapter 6. The governments being lobbied were supporting fossil fuels with trillions of dollars a year. Two hundred million is a small payment to protect that.", "https://influencemap.org/report/How-Big-Oil-Continues-to-Oppose-the-Paris-Agreement-38212275958aa21196dae3b76220bddc",
     add={"United Kingdom": 0, "United States": 0, "France": 0}, arcs=[("Houston", "Washington"), ("London", "Brussels"), ("Paris", "Brussels")], pins=[("London", "Shell, BP"), ("Paris", "Total"), ("Brussels", "EU lobbying")], pl=["exxon", "shell", "chevron", "bp", "total", "influencemap", "api"], ex=["im200"]),
   S("2024", "Sacramento", "The Same Method for Plastic", "Regional Example: In September 2024 the state of California sued ExxonMobil, the largest maker of the raw material for single-use plastic. The state's case is that for decades the company and its trade associations promoted recycling as the answer to plastic waste while knowing that most plastic could not be recycled at a profit and would be buried, burned or dumped. A 2024 report by the Center for Climate Integrity had gathered industry documents making the same point. ExxonMobil denies the claims and the case has not been decided. If the state is right, the purpose was the one described in this chapter: a public that believes in recycling does not demand a law limiting how much plastic is made.", "https://oag.ca.gov/news/press-releases/attorney-general-bonta-sues-exxonmobil-deceiving-public-recyclability-plastic",
     add={"United States": 0}, arcs=[("Houston", "Sacramento")], pins=[("Sacramento", "California v. ExxonMobil, 2024")], pl=["exxon", "acc"], reg=True, z=3.0),
  ],
  src=[{"t": "Supran, Rahmstorf and Oreskes, Assessing ExxonMobil's global warming projections, Science (2023)", "u": "https://www.science.org/doi/10.1126/science.abk0063"}])
